<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Halle (Saale)</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Halle an der Saale</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Halle is a city on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saale">Saale</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony-anhalt">Saxony-Anhalt</a>, long known for its salt springs. Ruled by the archbishops of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magdeburg">Magdeburg</a> until 1680, it then passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg-prussia">Brandenburg-Prussia</a>, whose new university, founded in 1694, became a centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pietism">Pietism</a> and the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Enlightenment</a>. The Francke Foundations and the birthplace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-frideric-handel">George Frideric Handel</a> are among its landmarks.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/halle-saale.jpg" alt="Halle (Saale)" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Vincent Eisfeld · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Blick_Marktkirche_Halle.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
